Data services (not redistributed)

ArWen downloads or templates requests for data from NOAA (GFS via the
NOMADS filter service and AWS Open Data; HRRR via AWS Open Data), from
the Copernicus Climate Data Store (ERA5; use is subject to the
Copernicus license and requires the user's own account), and relies on
the user-staged NCAR WPS geographical dataset (WPS_GEOG) for static
fields. None of these datasets are redistributed by this repository;
their terms are between the user and the provider.

`gpuwm fetch-geog` downloads WPS_GEOG by default from a byte-for-byte
mirror of the NCAR tarballs (tools/publish_geog_mirror.py writes its
provenance page). Two of its sources carry terms of their own:

  * soilgrids.tar.bz2 (the Noah-MP soil archive, `gpuwm mesh` only) is
    derived from SoilGrids250m by ISRIC -- World Soil Information (Hengl
    T. et al. 2017, "SoilGrids250m: Global gridded soil information based
    on machine learning", PLOS ONE 12(2): e0169748), licensed CC BY 4.0
    (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/); NCAR generated the
    WPS tiles and the mirror does not modify them.
  * soiltype_top_30s and soiltype_bot_30s use the FAO-UNESCO Soil Map of
    the World outside the United States (STATSGO, USDA public domain,
    inside it). FAO applies CC BY 4.0 to its datasets unless their own
    metadata says otherwise; the Digital Soil Map of the World catalogue
    record states no licence. Credit: FAO/UNESCO Soil Map of the World.

With `[static.highres]` enabled, ArWen downloads high-resolution static
sources into the user's own cache_root; none is redistributed here and
each run's receipt carries its source's attribution. The default land
cover carries terms of its own:

  * CGLC-MODIS-LCZ, the 100 m global land cover WRF and WPS ship from
    version 4.5:
    Demuzere M., He C., Martilli A. and Zonato A. (2023),
    doi:10.5281/zenodo.7670653, licensed CC BY 4.0
    (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). It is built from the
    Copernicus Global Land Service LC100 v3 (Buchhorn et al. 2020) and
    the global Local Climate Zone map (Demuzere et al. 2022, Earth Syst.
    Sci. Data 14, 3835). ArWen fetches the published GeoTIFF whole from
    Zenodo, unmodified, and maps its classes into WRF's MODIS categories
    at preparation time.


